{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nReplaying a candidate change tells you *what would change*, not whether it is *better*. The store already carries outcome signals that need no LLM or human labeling: revise→re-review pairs (2,483; per-finding survival via `norm_id` = \"author fixed it\" proxy), verdict flips on byte-identical material (208; the reviewer-noise floor), bug closes with `close_class=plan_defect` (84), `caused_by` links (89), tickets that PASSed plan review then FAILed completion (199), and reopens (18). `docs/experiments/calibrate_plan_review_thresholds.py` (`rev_rr`) and `docs/experiments/escaped_bug_analysis.py` compute pieces of this ad hoc; `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/harnesses/mine_outcome_corpus.py` separately freezes `reopen_count`/`force_close`/`completion_verifier_fail_count`/`rev_rr` per ticket into `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/runs/outcome_corpus.jsonl` — this ticket's ticket-level labels overlap that miner's fields and should read them rather than recompute, where the definitions agree.\n\n`src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/corpus.py` (ticket celestine-treasonous-ewe, closed) already exists: `build_corpus(store_roots, *, cache_dir) -> manifest` recovers PASS/BLOCK/INDETERMINATE `plan_review_result_v1/v2` sidecars via a git-object-walk (`_enumerate_event_blobs` + `_batch_cat`, deduped by `(ticket_id, uuid)`), reconstructs each sidecar's at-review material, verifies it against the stored `material_fingerprint`, and returns a manifest dict whose `content_hash` field (sha256 of the sorted-key JSON of the sorted verified rows) is THE corpus identity this ticket keys labels to — not a new hash invented here. The compact per-row cache `build_corpus` writes (keyed by that `content_hash`, under `cache_dir`) carries only the *summarized* per-sidecar row (schema, verdict, material_fingerprint, verified/generation, `ran_model`, timestamps) — it does **not** carry each sidecar's full `data` body (the Pass-1 findings array, Pass-2 answer vectors, Pass-3 outputs), because that summary is all `build_corpus`'s own fingerprint-verification job needs.\n\n## Approach\n\nBuild `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/labels.py`:\n\n- **Corpus identity (fixes \"keyed to a hash with no producer\")**: call `corpus.build_corpus(store_roots, cache_dir=...)` (same `store_roots`/`cache_dir` shape as celestine's ticket) to obtain the manifest; `manifest[\"content_hash\"]` IS the corpus manifest hash labels are keyed to. `labels.py` takes the same `store_roots`/`cache_dir` arguments and refuses (raises, naming both hashes) if a caller-supplied `labels-<hash>.jsonl` was built against a `content_hash` that does not match a freshly recomputed `build_corpus(...)[\"content_hash\"]` — this IS the \"loader refuses on mismatch\" AC.\n- **Finding-level source (fixes \"no producer for per-finding history\")**: finding-level labels need each sidecar's FULL `data` body (findings array, `norm_id` per finding, per-finding `material_fingerprint` where present), not the summarized corpus row. Reuse `corpus.py`'s own already-tested git-object-walk primitives directly — `corpus._enumerate_event_blobs(tracker_path)` + `corpus._batch_cat(tracker_path, shas)` — to re-read the SAME recovered `REVIEW_RESULT` blobs' raw JSON `data` (filtered to `kind == \"REVIEW_RESULT\"` and `data.get(\"schema\") in {\"plan_review_result_v1\", \"plan_review_result_v2\"}`, matching `corpus._SCHEMAS`), rather than a second, independent recovery implementation or an under-specified \"the store event logs\" claim. This is the same blob set `build_corpus` already verified is recoverable and deduped; `labels.py` does not re-derive reachability, only re-projects the full body instead of the summary.\n- **Ticket-level labels**: `escaped_defect` (later `plan_defect` bug or `caused_by` target), `completion_failed_after_pass`, `reopened`, `clean_close` (closed, no escape signals, closed ≥7 days before the corpus build — `CLEAN_CLOSE_MIN_DAYS = 7`, a named module constant, not a bare literal). Read `reopen_count`/`completion_verifier_fail_count`/`force_close` from `mine_outcome_corpus.py`'s frozen `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/runs/outcome_corpus.jsonl` where its field definitions match (documented per-label); compute only what that artifact does not carry (`escaped_defect` via `plan_defect`/`caused_by`, `clean_close`). Each label carries its source event id(s).\n- **Finding-level labels**: for consecutive reviews (from the finding-level source above) with changed material, join findings by `norm_id`; `resolved_by_author` (present in review k, absent in k+1 with material changed) vs `persisted`; `noise_flip` for identical-material pairs.\n- **Noise floor**: over identical-material pairs (same `material_fingerprint` across consecutive reviews) compute verdict flip rate, per-criterion finding churn (Jaccard by `norm_id`), and per-question Pass-2 answer agreement; publish as the band a candidate change must exceed.\n- Output `labels-<content_hash>.jsonl` plus a Markdown report with counts per label source, joined to `content_hash` so labels and corpus never drift apart.\n\nAssume the label definitions above as defaults; they are constants in one module so a later ticket can tighten them.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/labels.py` (new)\n- `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/replay/labels-report-<date>.md` (new)\n- `tests/unit/evals/test_plan_replay_labels.py` (new)\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit: synthetic corpus of five tickets exercising each ticket label and each finding label; identical-material pair with a verdict flip counted as noise, changed-material pair not; a ticket with both `plan_defect` and completion FAIL labelled once per source. Empty corpus → empty report. A `labels-<hash>.jsonl` built against a stale `content_hash` is refused by the loader when re-verified against a rebuilt corpus.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every review and finding in the corpus carries the labels above, with the source event ids; checked by the labels report counts matching independent `jq` counts over the store for `plan_defect`, `caused_by` and reopen events.\n- [ ] The noise floor (verdict flip rate, per-criterion churn, per-question agreement) is published with the identical-material pair count it was computed from; checked in the report.\n- [ ] Labels are keyed to `corpus.build_corpus(...)[\"content_hash\"]` and the loader raises when a fresh recompute of that hash disagrees with a supplied `labels-<hash>.jsonl`'s hash; checked by a unit test.\n- [ ] No LLM calls; checked by running with no provider credentials."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788059885547592990,"uuid":"a628a6c5-a257-4f7b-bf8f-e6efb1aab37e"}